{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '311,340p' collab/1792329904305-architect-register-wasm-lower-plan-r4.gpt-6-astra.findings.md; git status --short\"","aggregated_output":"## 5. Modules and code growth\n\nCompile each independently admitted image into a wasm Module whose body entry functions import the same ABI memory and services. Instantiate it for the receiving task and install its functions into the task's imported function table using the runtime embedding API. This table maps implementation body ids to uniform functions; it is not the guest offset table.\n\nAttach is a staged, append-only transaction:\n\n1. Apply the same contract/validator gates as `attach-image`. Empty images are no-ops; duplicate component R is a no-op. Production admission retains the existing A-to-R derivation check before executable attachment.\n2. Compute offset = held instruction count. Relocate every operand whose opcode-table kind is `:pc`; shift body start/end. Compute the exact combined segment and `register-hash`, and append `[component-R offset length]`.\n3. Compile/instantiate the new component, reserve directory/table capacity, install its body entries, then publish the new code-layout generation atomically. Prefer component-relative constants plus an instance base offset so the same compiled Module can serve different task layouts. Every pc generated from a component-relative coordinate adds the instance base exactly once before entering public state: header/exit pc, payload site/next pc, return-frame site/return pc, and minted closure body-pc. Incoming absolute restore/return pcs are already absolute and must not receive another base. Resolve absolute pcs through the combined body directory and subtract the selected body start for local dispatch.\n4. Preserve pc, registers, frames, K, all pending payloads, stores, and queues. No existing function slot or instruction pc is renumbered; old compiled instances remain reachable. Failure before publish leaves the old task executable. Attach only while wasm is at a materialized boundary.\n\nFor every boundary payload, fetch the instruction as `(nth (:instructions (:segment vm)) (:pc vm))` from the current relocated **combined** segment at the materialized absolute site pc. Pass that instruction and current segment/hash/images to `effects/continuation-payload`; never pair component-relative site metadata or a component-only segment with absolute payload pcs. The cached component image is compilation input, not the current capture-time code space. Even where the live operand is numerically unchanged by relocation, all payload coordinates and validation context must agree.\n\nAn empty-image creation seeds `:images [[empty-R 0 0]]`, `:hash nil`, `:pc 0`, `:registers []`, and `:halted? true`. Attaching a nonempty image of length n at offset 0 keeps the empty row and produces `[[empty-R 0 0] [R 0 n]]`; it does not unhalt the task, resize registers, or initialize a body. Reset or a valid execution-entry transition performs that separate work. A later capture includes `:images` because the row count is two, even though only one row contains instructions. Keep the zero-length row in payload validation and layout operations; it is distinct from the rule that attaching an empty image is a no-op.\n\nThere is no machine-code patching and no need to grow an existing wasm Module's function section. The baseline preallocates table capacity (W0 test profile: 65,536 slots) and uses host table installation plus directory append for new functions; exhaustion is a qualified resource refusal. No guest `table.grow`/`table.set` instruction is required. Optional host-API growth must pass the W0 probe on each runtime and must not change the baseline module feature profile. Dynamic instantiation/attachment occurs outside an active exchange after returning a staged host-work result; the next synchronized state publishes the new layout. The same component bytes and import names are used on all runtimes. Old call frames use absolute return-pc and resolve in the grown directory. Captures retain their old segment/hash for validation, but resume sees the current code space. Reset preserves offset rows as in the kernel; load-image replaces the code space and resets execution, so old continuation membership checks can fail.\n\n`spawn-module` creates a fresh task/memory/arena/owner/counter/resources context, with exactly the composition options copied by the existing kernel. It does not borrow the parent's mutable register shadow or per-module store. Reuse the module scheduler child-task lifecycle, encoder/decoder and `module/IModuleKernel` seams. Closure lifting uses image-relative body references and store-of; lowering validates binding/format, attached origin, and arity against actual closure instructions before minting the receiver's trusted value. Module store snapshots and authenticated stream/cursor lowering remain engine work. Cache compiled code by A, r2 derivation/checksum, wasm ABI/emitter version and feature profile; R alone is not executable admission authority.\n\n## 6. Implementation slices and acceptance gates\n\n### Test harness common to every slice\n\nBuild an explicit differential adapter rather than replacing the existing tests' private `register-run` vars by global mutation. Parameterize/extract shared fixtures when implementation is authorized. Each case receives independent scripted streams with identical inputs, capability secret, primitive registry, origin and counter setup. From W1 onward, compile each image once and run those identical wasm bytes under **Node and Wasmtime**, each against interpreted register after every guest instruction and scheduler event; Wasmer must also pass the complete suite by W5. Compare serialized ABI traffic as well as full semantic traces; also compare semantic execution where the existing A→R fragment permits it.\n\nUse `test/yin/vm/semantic/corpus.cljc` **programs and literal goldens**: project AST to A, assert the literal vector/address, derive R, validate R/live sets, compile and instantiate wasm. These goldens specify syntax/address, not a runnable environment or expected final value for every entry. Supply deterministic f/g/h/k/x/y/c/loop bindings, stream fixtures, and genuine parked targets for resume cases; otherwise compare the same qualified refusal. Execute both conditional arms where applicable. Do not silently skip resume/terminal/closure cases or relabel unsupported cases as successes.\n\nUse all fixtures/assertions of `test/yin/vm/debruijn/register_test.cljc`, including hand images, B0/B2 parity, stream scripts, continuation corruption, and IVM behavior. B0 normalization is suitable for final cross-model values but is insufficient for this backend: additionally compare raw register payload shapes, stores/module-stores, pc, frame order, K, counter, flags, wait/ready/parked entries and emitted request traces. Compare resources by authenticated logical identity/cursor progress and stream emissions, not by host object address. With identical secrets and inputs, hash/owner/seal/profile/address strings, guest-visible ids and slice ordering must be bit-identical. Compare canonical expanded observable-state encodings, not pointer layouts; private arena numbers may be renamed only where they cannot affect key ordering, id minting or guest state. Across independent tasks use the same explicit owner inputs; never erase owner checks. Test primitive implementations and scripted transports exist in both native and CLJS compositions with the same declared profiles; a JS function fixture must get an equivalent native fixture, not disappear from the native run. Normalize only private memory addresses, arena numbering and runtime object identities; preserve all guest values, errors, pcs, frames, snapshots, queues, counters, store/heap/custody changes, resource progress and emitted request order. Compare failures by qualified rule/reason/outcome and relevant message assertions.\n\n### W0 — freeze the ABI and executable oracle\n M docs/agents/delegate-invocation-reference.md\n?? bin/claude-cloud\n?? collab/1791307819601-architect-dao-star-gaps.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md\n?? collab/1791307819601-architect-dao-star-gaps.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log\n?? collab/1791307819601-architect-dao-star-gaps.prompt.md\n?? collab/1791308400000-reviewer-dao-star-gaps-rulings.glm-5-3.findings.md\n?? collab/1791308400000-reviewer-dao-star-gaps-rulings.glm-5-3.stdout.log\n?? collab/1791308400000-reviewer-dao-star-gaps-rulings.gpt-6-1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1791308400000-reviewer-dao-star-gaps-rulings.prompt.md\n?? collab/1791309600000-architect-dao-star-apply-rulings.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log\n?? collab/1791309600000-architect-dao-star-apply-rulings.prompt.md\n?? collab/1791316000000-reviewer-dao-star-edited-docs.gpt-6-1-sol.findings.md\n?? collab/1791316000000-reviewer-dao-star-edited-docs.gpt-6-1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1791316000000-reviewer-dao-star-edited-docs.prompt.md\n?? collab/1791316800000-architect-dao-star-fix-review.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log\n?? collab/1791316800000-architect-dao-star-fix-review.prompt.md\n?? collab/1791317400000-reviewer-dao-star-edited-docs-r2.gpt-6-1-sol.findings.md\n?? collab/1791317400000-reviewer-dao-star-edited-docs-r2.gpt-6-1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1791317400000-reviewer-dao-star-edited-docs-r2.prompt.md\n?? collab/1791318000000-architect-dao-star-fix-r2.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log\n?? collab/1791318000000-architect-dao-star-fix-r2.prompt.md\n?? collab/1791318600000-reviewer-dao-star-edited-docs-r3.gpt-6-1-sol.findings.md\n?? collab/1791318600000-reviewer-dao-star-edited-docs-r3.gpt-6-1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1791318600000-reviewer-dao-star-edited-docs-r3.prompt.md\n?? collab/1791366000000-compiler-engineer-ucf-d15-fix-continue.prompt.md\n?? collab/1791370000000-vm-engineer-ucf-d16prep.prompt.md\n?? collab/1791370000000-writer-ucf-doc-batch.prompt.md\n?