ppers. It is never a parallel port of register execution, scheduler, validator, primitive semantics, custody, module stores, collection or FFI two-step. The facade decodes validated messages, resolves checked handles, invokes the shared transition functions and serializes their committed outcomes. It owns only transport/arena/runtime mechanics. The Node adapter decodes into existing CLJS records and calls existing CLJC helpers. Native execution cannot be replay, a JS subprocess or success-only stubs; missing required semantics fails the slice.\n\nThe “one transition implementation, two dispatchers” rule (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:991–995, 1308–1311`) applies across these execution strategies: CLJS kernel and CLJS wasm adapter reach the same CLJC transition; Rust interpreter, native wasm facade and later LLVM helpers reach the same Rust transition. Across CLJC and Rust there is necessarily a language port, governed by one specification and one conformance kit—not literally one callable binary. Within either host, a second service-specific transition implementation is a defect. Generated constants/moves/branches specialize the admitted instruction operands; generated service sites never reproduce call/return/restore/effect logic in a wrapper. Extract reusable transition helpers in the owning crate if necessary, rather than dispatching a second copy of the engine.\n\nPreserve check-then-commit atomicity and fault containment (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:639–713`): refused transitions leave guest state unchanged; a panic is a kernel defect, caught before crossing the runtime boundary, poisons only its machine, and cannot become a success payload. ABI malformed-message refusals precede semantic work; retained responses preserve once-only effect completion. Safe facade code uses checked slices and Value operations. Do not expand the unsafe surface beyond the Rust design’s explicit boundary crates (lines 1154–1158); any unavoidable runtime binding belongs to a reviewed boundary, never the kernel/engine. Imported service calls are internal execution plumbing; guest external effects still use stream emissions, not arbitrary synchronous host callbacks.'\"'''+s[b:]\ns=s.replace('### 2.2 Structuring algorithm', '''### 2.1.1 LLVM sibling and shared equivalence law\n\nThe wasm path and Rust K4 LLVM path are sibling execution strategies under \"'`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:805–831` (§7.2): from equal reachable boundary states they reach equal later states, ordered effects and refusal diagnostics. Both use body functions, entry-pc dispatch, live sets as spill maps and data guest calls; their native control instructions differ, their guest semantics do not (§7.3, lines 833–918). W0’s shared kit tests all lanes; neither translator forks the validator, liveness rules, payload schema or semantic service implementation. An artifact may be discarded at a materialized boundary and execution continued by the owning interpreter from the same state. Add wasm→Rust interpreter and wasm→CLJS interpreter fallback fixtures; no artifact field may be needed to restore.\n\n### 2.2 Structuring algorithm'\"''',1)\ns=s.replace('## 6. Implementation slices and acceptance gates', '''Artifact policy is also shared with LLVM: \"'`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:997–1011, 1113–1122, 1261–1263`. Wasm binaries are disposable **node-local caches**, never dao.jing content identities, refs, continuation fields or portable module payloads. Include target/profile, translator and ABI versions in the cache key alongside the admitted A/R provenance above. No cross-node artifact fetching/sharing is introduced. A missing/dropped artifact falls back to the same host’s register interpreter; no compiler or LLVM worker is required to resume. Install derived artifacts only at materialized boundaries, with no pending mutation or active guest stack. Body-relative code and task base offsets follow the same row-local cache rule in both backends.\n\n## 6. Implementation slices and acceptance gates'\"''',1)\ns=s.replace('Build an explicit differential adapter rather than', '''**W0 and Rust K0 are the same conformance-kit deliverable**, exported from R4, not independent harness authorities (\"'`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1185–1199`). Consume and extend its canonical images, R, B0-normalized results/errors, boundary payloads/K depth/saved pairs, descriptor/scalar vectors, ordered validator refusals/messages and refused-step atomicity fixtures. Preserve its tail-site matrix, >65,535-register, host-limit and million-tail fixtures. If K0 exports are not yet available, create that shared deliverable once; do not fabricate a wasm-only reference interpreter. Record the actual reference revision and fixture version, including the explicit shared traversal stabilization delta.\n\nOne manifest drives **three execution lanes: R4 CLJS reference, Rust kernel/interpreter, wasm**. Wasm runs the same bytes under Node and native runtimes as gated below; all use the same scripted inputs, scalar/host-limit declarations and expected refusals. ABI-specific probes extend K0 as a transport test group without replacing format-level fixtures. Rust interpreter coverage advances with each W slice; it cannot be omitted because native wasm happens to pass. B0 results and complete boundary traces remain shared; wasm single-step traces add finer evidence, not a second oracle.\n\nBuild an explicit differential adapter rather than'\"''',1)\ns=s.replace('Deliver CLJS and native test adapters,', 'Deliver/extend K0’s single exported fixture kit and its CLJS, Rust-interpreter and wasm adapters,',1)\ns=s.replace('### W1 — pure register execution and body dispatch', '''W/K dependency map: W0 consumes/extends K0; W1 shares K1 pure kernel/value/image work; W2 shares K2 payload/restore work plus the required \"'`yin-engine` park subset; W3–W4 require the Level-2 library services described in Rust §6.3, not a second engine. W5 is wasm release qualification, not certification of the full K5 standalone node. W6 can contribute wasm measurements to K3’s same corpus/harness; K4 still requires the owner’s review of K3 (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1167–1182, 1215–1228`). No dependency authorizes unrelated implementation in this documentation task.\n\n### W1 — pure register execution and body dispatch'\"''',1)\ns=s.replace('provide native value, primitive, store and execution-state services in this slice.', 'provide those services through the shared Rust crates and require the Rust interpreter to pass the same pure fixtures. Also test refused-step atomicity and per-machine fault isolation from K0/K1, including the wide-register and million-tail cases.',1)\ns=s.replace('This optional service migration is outside W5’s implementation,', 'Any service migration reuses/compiles the same Rust crate semantics where the toolchain/profile permits it, rather than authoring a third engine; the i32-handle ABI and full kit remain the contract. K3 may measure wasm on this same harness alongside its JVM/Rust/AOT comparisons; these measurements do not commission K4. This optional service migration is outside W5’s implementation,',1)\ns=s.replace('### Explicit non-goals\\\\n\\\\n', '### Explicit non-goals\\\\n\\\\nNo second Rust kernel/engine/value/codec port; no K4 LLVM implementation, full K5 standalone node, new Rust network transport, cross-node artifact service or Rust Rc-layout exposure is authorized by this wasm plan.\\\\n\\\\n',1)\ns=s.replace('10. **Host coverage.**', '10. **Single-port dependency.** Native coverage depends on the shared Rust crates, including the substantial Level-2 engine subset needed before W3/W4. A faster parallel port is not a workaround. Keep one transition implementation per host language, one exported K0/W0 kit and explicit shared-reference versioning; duplicate dispatchers must not duplicate semantic code. The Value facade and snapshot barriers must preserve check-then-commit and Rc ownership while keeping handles task-local.\\\\n11. **Host coverage.**',1)\ns=s.replace('Reference convention:', '- **r5:** reconciled the Rust-kernel revision-2 authority, single Rust service port, fixed native Value facade, shared K0/W0 kit and LLVM sibling/cache law; explicitly superseded only the wasm-artifact deferral and documented the scope/identity/index/step/Level-2 scheduling reconciliations. Prior response sections remain historical evidence.\\\\n\\\\nReference convention:',1)\ns=s.replace('Only this r4 findings file was written. Source checks and document coverage checks were performed; implementation/runtime acceptance tests remain planned, not claimed as executed.', 'For the historical r4 revision only its findings file was written. Its source and document checks did not execute implementation/runtime acceptance tests.',1)\ns+='''\n\n## Round 5 changes\n\n1. Added Rust-kernel revision 2 as a native-design authority, verified its 1,333-line document in full, retained its design-only status and K4 commissioning gate, and explicitly superseded its DEFERRED “WASM as an artifact target” item. Recorded C1/A3/owner-question alignment without reopening held-image translation.\n2. Required the native service to be the Rust design’s crates plus a handle/ABI facade; Wasmtime/Wasmer wrappers contain no parallel semantic port. Applied one-transition/two-dispatchers reuse within each language, with one conformance law across CLJC and Rust, and retained atomicity/fault isolation and safe ownership.\n3. Specified task-local i32 handle→16-byte repr(C,u8), Rc-counted Value ownership, checked generations/no reuse and safe Clone/Drop; no Rust layout or pointer crosses the ABI. Preserved canonical expanded-byte ordering, canonical NaN with integral floats/-0.0, and unambiguous framing unchanged. Assigned repository codecs/hashes to the shared crates.\n4. Made W0 and K0 one deliverable exported from R4, extended by ABI/portability cases, consumed by CLJS, Rust interpreter and wasm lanes. Retained W0–W6, toolchain pins, Node/Wasmtime full traces, W4 Wasmer W1–W4 and bidirectional module-slice gates. Added shared refused-step/fault/large-register/tail/fallback checks and a W/K dependency map.\n5. Made wasm and K4 LLVM siblings under §7.2 and the same node-local artifact-cache law: no identity, payload or cross-node artifact fetch; interpreter fallback at boundaries; no fork in validator, live sets, kit or semantics. K3 may measure wasm using that harness; K4 remains separately gated.\n6. Explicitly reconciled older R-identity language, u32 native limits versus 64-bit wire pcs, Rust interpreter-only public step versus wasm inspection stepping, no-CLJC-edit K-phase scope versus r4’s separate shared-ordering prerequisite, and early Level-2 library needs versus the full K5 node. No silent changes to the Rust authority or earlier findings files.\n\nValidation: the Rust design was read in full and the new section/line references checked against its actual text; existing source citations and prior review-response tables were preserved. All seven plan sections, all 23 opcode rows, W0–W6 and revision history remain. This revision writes only the r5 findings file; runtime tests and implementation remain planned work, not reported results.\n'''\nout=p.with_name(p.name.replace('-r4.','-r5.'))\nassert not out.exists(), out\nout.write_text(s)\nprint(f'Wrote {out}: {len(s.splitlines())} lines, {len(s.split())} words')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"Wrote collab/1792329904305-architect-register-wasm-lower-plan-r5.gpt-6-astra.findings.md: 544 lines, 16551 words\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
